KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ pointer variable: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pointer member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^Invariant check failed$
--
N5008 [dcl.typedef]/2: `using F = void(int);' declares the same type as
`typedef void F(int);'.  With the alias, `F *p = &cb;' violates the
invariant "symbol type must match" (cpp_typecheck_initializer.cpp), and
assigning &cb to a member of type F * is a conversion error ("invalid
implicit conversion from 'auto (*)(signed int) -> void' to 'auto
(*)(signed int) -> void'"); with the typedef both work.  libc++'s
__stop_callback_base declares `using __callback_fn_t =
void(__stop_callback_base*) noexcept;', so the body of
__stop_callback_base::__invoke is dropped (__stop_token/stop_state.h:33).
Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it
fails only its WRONG assertion.
